course: everyday
lesson: 256
title: Questions, group work and the adults at school
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Asking a question is the fastest way to learn, and teachers like questions; a question that is not about the lesson can wait until break or the end of the lesson.
explain: In group work every idea deserves to be heard: everyone takes a turn, disagreements are settled fairly by listening and voting, and if your idea is not chosen you help with the one that was.
explain: Many adults look after you at school: teachers and teaching assistants teach, the office staff contact families, the nurse or first aider helps when you are hurt, and the caretaker, cleaners and lunch staff keep the school running.
explain: You call teachers Mr, Mrs, Ms or Miss with their surname, or Sir and Miss in many British schools.
explain: A boy in Austin who is worried about his parents arguing tells his teacher, and the worry feels lighter; but if an adult ever asks him to keep a secret that makes him uncomfortable, he tells a parent or another trusted adult.
fact: Teachers like questions, and asking is the fastest way to learn, even if you fear the question is silly.
fact: A question that is not about the lesson is saved for break time or the end of the lesson.
fact: In a group you work well with everyone, even children you do not usually play with.
fact: The best group results come when everyone takes a turn and every idea is heard.
fact: When a group disagrees, it listens to each idea, then votes or tries a mix of ideas.
fact: If the group does not choose your idea, you accept it and help with the chosen one.
fact: When a partner makes a mistake, you help them see it kindly, without making them feel bad, because kind help makes people willing to keep trying.
fact: To present to the class, you practise beforehand, speak slowly and loudly, and look at the audience.
fact: Teachers are called Mr, Mrs, Ms or Miss with their surname, or Sir and Miss in many British schools.
fact: The teaching assistant or aide helps teach the class and is listened to like a teacher.
fact: The office staff help when you are late, hurt, ill or need to phone home, and the nurse or first aider helps when you are hurt or ill.
fact: Worries are lighter when shared with a trusted adult, such as a teacher, the school counsellor or a parent.
fact: Safe adults never ask children to keep secrets that worry them; such a secret is told to a parent or another trusted adult.
exercise: You do not understand fractions, but you think your question is silly. What should you do? | Ask anyway, because teachers like questions | Stay quiet so nobody laughs | Wait until next year to ask | Copy a friend's work instead
exercise: In a maths lesson you want to ask the teacher about her holiday. When should you ask? | At break time or the end of the lesson | Right now, in the middle of the lesson | Never, because it is not allowed | During the test, when it is quiet
exercise: You are put in a group with children you do not usually play with. What is best? | Be friendly and work together well | Ask the teacher to move you to your friends' group | Work alone and ignore them | Let them do all the work
exercise: In your group, one child does all the talking. What could you suggest? | That everyone takes a turn to share an idea | That the talker does the whole task alone, since he likes it | That nobody speaks at all | That the quietest child leaves
exercise: Your group cannot agree how to make a poster. What is a fair way to decide? | Listen to each idea, then vote or mix them | Let the loudest child decide | Stop working until the teacher decides | Each person makes their own poster secretly
exercise: The group chooses Maya's idea, not yours. What do you do? | Accept it and help with Maya's idea | Refuse to help with her idea | Do your own idea anyway | Tell the teacher the group is unfair
exercise: Your partner says 9 + 6 = 14. How do you help? | Kindly show them how to check it again | Laugh and tell the others | Say they are bad at maths | Rub out their answer and write yours
exercise: You must present your project to the class. What helps most? | Practise first, speak slowly and look at the audience | Read quickly from the paper without ever looking up | Speak quietly so mistakes are not heard | Hope it goes well without practising, as it usually does
exercise: How do you usually address your teacher, Mrs Patel, in a US or Australian school? | Mrs Patel | Priya | Hey, teacher | Patel
exercise: The teaching assistant asks you to sit down. What do you do? | Listen and sit down, as you would for a teacher | Ignore her, because she is not the real teacher of the class | Ask the real teacher first | Sit down only if your friends do
exercise: How should you treat the caretaker, cleaners and lunch staff? | Say thank you and help keep the school tidy | Leave mess, because cleaning is their job | Ignore them, as they do not teach | Be polite only when a teacher is watching
exercise: You arrive late and your mum has already left. Where do you go first? | To the school office or reception | Straight to the playground to find your friends | To hide in the toilets | Home again to wait for your mum
exercise: You twist your ankle at break. Who can help? | The school nurse or first aider | The other children in your class | Nobody, until you are back home after school | Only the lunch staff, who serve the food
exercise: You feel sad because your pet died. What could you do at school? | Tell a trusted adult, such as your teacher | Keep it to yourself so that nobody at school worries | Pretend to be fine all week | Tell only your toy
exercise: An adult at school gives you sweets and says, "Don't tell your parents about our secret." What do you do? | Do not keep it; tell a parent or trusted adult | Keep the secret, because an adult asked | Ask a friend whether to keep it | Keep it but stop taking sweets
test: A girl in Dublin is afraid her question about long division sounds silly. What is true? | Asking is the quickest way to learn, and teachers welcome it | Only the clever children in the class are allowed to ask | Questions make the teacher cross | She should wait until she works it out alone, however long
test: In a group of four in Toronto, Sam always speaks first and the others stay quiet. What gives the best result? | Everyone takes a turn so every idea is heard | Sam does all the deciding because he is fast | The quiet ones write down Sam's ideas | The group splits up and works alone
test: Two group members argue: one wants a model, the other a poster. What is a fair way forward? | Listen to both, then vote or combine them | The older one wins, because older means wiser | Whoever shouts loudest wins | They stop working until home time
test: In a British school, what might children call their male teacher? | Sir | His first name | Mate | Teacher Smith
test: Why should you treat the classroom aide like a teacher? | She helps teach and look after the class | She is the head teacher's sister | She will give you sweets if you behave | She is only there to help one child and nobody else
test: A boy in Phoenix feels ill after lunch and wants to call his dad. Where should he go? | The school office or the nurse | The bike shed, where it is quiet | Back to class to keep it quiet and say nothing | The playground to wait for his dad
test: Why is it right to tell a parent if an adult asks you to keep an uncomfortable secret? | Safe adults never ask children to keep worrying secrets | Adults always want secrets told to parents | It is fun to tell secrets | You will get a prize for telling
test: Before giving a talk, a boy in Auckland feels very nervous. What helps him most? | Practising the talk beforehand | Talking very fast to get it over with | Looking only at the floor so he does not see faces | Whispering so nobody can hear mistakes
test: Your partner in Edinburgh gets an answer wrong and looks embarrassed. Why help kindly? | Kind help makes them willing to keep trying | Kind help makes the teacher give you a prize | They will give you their answers later | It is the only way to finish quickly
test: wanna ask the teacher smth but it's a dumb question prob | Ask it, teachers like questions | Don't ask, it's silly and everyone will laugh at you | Ask a friend to ask for you | Wait till next year
test: group picked jo's idea not mine, ugh | Accept it and help with the chosen idea | Stop working with the group | Do your own idea alone and show them it's better | Complain to everyone
test: man said keep it secret, don't tell your mum, it's worrying me | Tell a parent or another trusted adult | Keep it, a promise is a promise | Tell nobody ever | Only tell your diary
test: presenting to the class tmrw, scared. tips? | Practise first, speak slowly, look at the audience | Read very fast so you finish as quickly as you can | Look at the floor the whole time | Whisper so nobody hears mistakes
explain: A boy in Texas called Leo did not understand fractions but was too embarrassed to ask; by the end of the year he could not follow any of the lessons that built on them.
explain: A girl in Ireland felt unwell at school but told no adult and walked home alone; she fainted on the way, and her parents did not know where she was.
fact: Lessons build on one another, so a gap you never ask about grows bigger over time.
fact: If you are ill or hurt at school, telling a school adult means someone can look after you and call home.
test: dunno how to do this but don't wanna look dumb asking | Ask the teacher, small gaps grow big | Just guess and move on, nobody will notice | Copy your neighbour, nobody will ever know | Wait for next year when it gets explained again
test: feel really sick, gonna just walk home | Tell a teacher or the office first | Walk home quickly | Hide in the toilets | Text a friend and leave
test: Why did Leo get lost in the fraction lessons later on? | He never asked about the part he missed | The lessons were taught in another language | He was too clever for them and got bored | Fractions are only for adults and big children
test: did u hear some adult at a club told a kid to keep a secret from his parents and the kid got really worried? real lesson? | Safe adults don't ask for worrying secrets; tell someone | Keep the secret because an adult said so | Only tell if the adult says you can, otherwise keep quiet | Secrets with adults are always fine
test: apparently that boy asked a silly question in maths and the whole class laughed so hard he never spoke again. what's probably exaggerated? | Never spoke again; and questions are how you learn | That some of the other children laughed at him | That he asked a question | That maths was the lesson
test: my mate says the teaching assistant isn't a real teacher so u don't have to listen to her. right? | No, the assistant helps teach and is listened to | Yes, only the main teacher counts | Yes, assistants have no rules and can't tell anyone off | Yes, unless she shouts
exam: Identify the appropriate response where a group disagrees upon a plan. | Each idea is heard, then a vote or a mixture is tried | The loudest member decides | The group ceases to work until the teacher intervenes on its behalf | The member with most friends decides
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a secret that an adult asks a child to keep and that worries him? | It is to be told to a parent or another trusted adult | It is to be kept forever | It is to be told only to other children, never to adults | It is to be forgotten
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice for presenting to a class. | One practises, speaks slowly and loudly, and looks at the audience | One reads quickly, looking only at the paper | One speaks quietly so as not to disturb | One improvises without any preparation whatsoever, trusting to luck
exam: Ascertain the reason for which pupils ask about gaps in their understanding. | Lessons build upon one another, so gaps grow | Teachers are offended by silent pupils | Questions earn extra marks in the final examination | Gaps disappear without any help
